Subject: [PATCH 8/8] SDL: drive the GL main loop from the browser's event loop

PPSSPP's OpenGL path makes the thread that owns the GL context the render
thread, and that thread never comes back: main() runs `while (true)` around
graphicsContext->ThreadFrame(), which is GLRenderManager::ThreadFrame(), which
waits on a condition variable until the emu thread submits a frame.

That is correct on every platform where the render thread is a thread of our
own. In a browser it is the thread the page also needs for input, for
compositing, and for the WebGL swap — which only happens when control returns to
the event loop. Holding it means no frame is ever presented and the tab stops
responding.

Measured rather than assumed: a browser harness installs a heartbeat on the main
thread before callMain() and asks for it afterwards from outside. Before this
patch the page stops answering at the callMain stage, having got as far as
creating its window and registering its event handlers.

The loop body is lifted into `oneIteration()` and is otherwise untouched — the
two `break`s become `return false`, and nothing else moves. Native builds run it
in the same `while` as before. Emscripten hands it to
emscripten_set_main_loop at 0 fps, which means requestAnimationFrame: the
browser's own clock, and the right one for a renderer.

The GL context stays on the main thread, which is where a browser wants it.
Moving main() to a worker with -sPROXY_TO_PTHREAD would take the whole render
thread with it and turn this into a WebGL-on-a-worker problem, needing
OffscreenCanvas or proxied GL; this needs neither.

simulate_infinite_loop is set, so main() stops at that call rather than falling
into the shutdown below, and Emscripten leaves the frame standing rather than
unwinding it — which is what keeps the captures in the callback valid.

diff --git a/SDL/SDLMain.cpp b/SDL/SDLMain.cpp
index c20b1303a..1bb399fd9 100644
--- a/SDL/SDLMain.cpp
+++ b/SDL/SDLMain.cpp
@@ -13,6 +13,11 @@ SDLJoystick *joystick = NULL;
 #include <bcm_host.h>
 #endif
 
+#ifdef __EMSCRIPTEN__
+#include <emscripten.h>
+#include <functional>
+#endif
+
 #include <atomic>
 #include <algorithm>
 #include <cmath>
@@ -2199,7 +2204,10 @@ int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
 			NativeFrame(graphicsContext);
 			return true;
 		});
-		while (true) {
+		// One turn of the loop, lifted out unchanged so that a platform which owns its
+		// own event loop can drive it a turn at a time. Returns false when the loop is
+		// finished, which is what the `break`s below used to mean.
+		auto oneIteration = [&]() -> bool {
 			// OpenGL mode uses this.
 			{
 				SDL_Event event;
@@ -2208,7 +2216,7 @@ int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
 				}
 			}
 			if (g_QuitRequested || g_RestartRequested)
-				break;
+				return false;
 
 			UpdateTextFocus(window);
 			UpdateSDLCursor();
@@ -2225,7 +2233,7 @@ int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
 					// and has now reached the end of the submitted frames.
 					// EmuThread will be in the process of exiting, so below
 					// we can just EmuThread_Join().
-					break;
+					return false;
 				}
 			}
 
@@ -2235,9 +2243,38 @@ int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
 					UpdateWindowState(window);
 				}
 			}
+			return true;
+		};
+
+#ifdef __EMSCRIPTEN__
+		// A browser's main thread is not ours to hold, and this loop holds it for the
+		// entire run: ThreadFrame() waits on a condition variable until the emu thread
+		// submits a frame. That is correct on every platform where the render thread is
+		// a thread of our own. Here it is also the thread the browser needs for events,
+		// for compositing, and for the WebGL swap that only happens when we return — so
+		// holding it means no frame is ever presented, and the tab stops.
+		//
+		// Same body, one turn per animation frame. Nothing above this line changes.
+		static std::function<void()> webIteration;
+		webIteration = [&]() {
+			if (!oneIteration()) {
+				emscripten_cancel_main_loop();
+				INFO_LOG(Log::System, "Requesting render thread exit...");
+				EmuThread_Join(graphicsContext, emuThread);
+			}
+		};
+		// 0 fps asks for requestAnimationFrame, which is the browser's own pace and the
+		// right clock for a renderer. The last argument makes this call throw rather
+		// than return, so main() stops here instead of falling into the shutdown below;
+		// Emscripten leaves the frame standing rather than unwinding it, which is what
+		// keeps the captures above valid for the callbacks.
+		emscripten_set_main_loop([]() { webIteration(); }, 0, 1);
+#else
+		while (oneIteration()) {
 		}
 		INFO_LOG(Log::System, "Requesting render thread exit...");
 		EmuThread_Join(graphicsContext, emuThread);
+#endif
 	}
 
 #if !PPSSPP_PLATFORM(MAC)
